WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.317
This runnable example makes row multiplication visible.

00:00:03.317 --> 00:00:08.012
We join patients to admissions, lab results, and prescriptions, all by patient_id.

00:00:08.012 --> 00:00:14.572
Each child table can have multiple rows for the same patient.

00:00:14.572 --> 00:00:19.442
Focus on a patient who has several records in more than one child table.

00:00:19.442 --> 00:00:23.797
The final joined row count can be much larger than the number of admissions, lab tests,

00:00:23.797 --> 00:00:27.477
or prescriptions that actually occurred.

00:00:27.477 --> 00:00:30.847
COUNT star counts the joined combinations.

00:00:30.847 --> 00:00:33.712
That is why it can look surprisingly large.

00:00:33.712 --> 00:00:40.322
COUNT DISTINCT admission_id, COUNT DISTINCT test_id, and COUNT DISTINCT prescription_id are trying to recover

00:00:40.322 --> 00:00:44.402
the number of original events from the multiplied result.

00:00:44.402 --> 00:00:46.472
In a real project, this can create serious reporting errors.

00:00:46.472 --> 00:00:52.962
Imagine a hospital dashboard that reports the number of admissions after admissions are joined to several lab

00:00:52.962 --> 00:00:59.247
and medication tables. If COUNT star is used carelessly, admission volume can be exaggerated.

00:00:59.247 --> 00:01:01.817
The query is not broken.

00:01:01.817 --> 00:01:07.252
It is counting something different from what the analyst intended.

00:01:07.252 --> 00:01:12.162
Whenever a count looks too large, ask what one joined row represents.

00:01:12.162 --> 00:01:15.145
That question often reveals the problem immediately.
